# PHASE 2 — ESTABLISH ONE CONSISTENT MAVLY DESIGN SYSTEM

The entire website must look like one coherent product, even when different pages serve different purposes.

The login page, homepage, dashboard, editor, settings, and other existing pages should not look as though they were created from unrelated templates.

## 1. Centralise and reuse existing design tokens

Use existing design tokens wherever possible.

Standardise the use of:

- Brand colours and neutral colours.
- Surface colours and backgrounds.
- Typography and text hierarchy.
- Font weights and line heights.
- Spacing and layout conventions.
- Border colours and widths.
- Corner radii.
- Shadows and elevation levels.
- Button dimensions.
- Icon sizing and alignment.
- Focus indicators.
- Motion timing and easing.
- Responsive breakpoints.

If an existing concept is represented inconsistently across multiple stylesheets, consolidate it carefully without causing unrelated regressions.

Avoid duplicated values and unnecessary new CSS variables.

## 2. Create a clear surface and elevation system

Use a restrained elevation scale consistent with Mavly's existing theme.

For example, establish reusable treatments for:

- Base page surfaces.
- Subtle inset surfaces.
- Standard cards and panels.
- Elevated menus and floating elements.
- Interactive buttons.
- Modals and overlays.

Use the actual theme colours to derive suitable shadow pairs.

Do not prescribe new surface colours merely to make the Neumorphic effect more visible.

Avoid low-contrast elements that become indistinguishable from their backgrounds.

## 3. Standardise typography

Review typography throughout the site.

Ensure that similar types of content use consistent:

- Font families.
- Font weights.
- Heading hierarchy.
- Body-text sizes.
- Line heights.
- Letter spacing.
- Text colours.
- Maximum readable line lengths.

Page-specific typography may vary according to its role, but it must remain part of the same design system.

Do not introduce unrelated fonts or styling conventions on individual pages.

## 4. Create reusable component variants

Where existing architecture allows, standardise the visual treatment of shared components.

Useful candidates include:

- Primary and secondary buttons.
- Tertiary and text buttons.
- Icon buttons.
- Links.
- Cards and panels.
- Form fields and labels.
- Select menus.
- Checkboxes and switches.
- Tabs and segmented controls.
- Accordions.
- Tooltips.
- Popovers.
- Dialogs and modals.
- Loading indicators.
- Toast notifications.

Use shared components and design tokens where practical.

Do not create a separate, visually different button implementation for every page.

Do not force every card or section to look identical. Different components can serve different purposes while still following the same spacing, typography, elevation, and interaction conventions.

## 5. Preserve page-specific structure

Consistency does not mean that every page must use the same layout.

The homepage can remain marketing-focused. The login page can remain authentication-focused. The editor can remain workspace-focused.

Standardise the shared visual language, component behaviour, and motion system without damaging the specific workflows of those pages.

Do not turn the entire application into one enormous layout or a collection of identical cards.

