# PHASE 4 — IMPLEMENT A CONSISTENT MOTION SYSTEM

The current website feels static because many pages open without an entrance transition, sections appear without visual continuity, and buttons often lack interaction feedback.

Create one coherent motion language and apply it throughout the frontend.

## 1. Establish shared motion tokens

Inspect whether the project already has motion tokens or transition conventions.

Reuse them where available.

If they do not exist, define a small, consistent set of reusable timing and easing values for:

- Micro-interactions.
- Button and control transitions.
- Dropdown and accordion transitions.
- Card and panel transitions.
- Page entrance transitions.
- Section reveal animations.

As starting guidance, short control interactions may take approximately 150–250 milliseconds, and page or section entrances may take approximately 300–500 milliseconds. Adapt these values to the existing application and the actual interaction.

Do not make every transition use an identical duration when different interactions require different behaviour.

Prefer smooth, restrained easing over abrupt movement or excessive bouncing.

Avoid exaggerated spring effects unless a specific component genuinely benefits from them.

## 2. Animate every existing page on entry

Every applicable page should have a consistent, subtle entry animation when it is first displayed through navigation.

Possible treatments include:

- A small opacity transition.
- A slight upward movement of the main page container.
- A restrained reveal of the primary content.
- A subtle transition of the existing navigation or page header.

Keep the animation short and unobtrusive.

The user should understand the page immediately.

Do not make visitors wait for the entire page to animate before they can interact.

Avoid large zooms, aggressive sliding, page spinning, or long cinematic introductions.

### Important route-transition requirements

- Preserve the current routing behaviour.
- Reuse the existing routing and animation infrastructure.
- Do not change protected-route behaviour or authentication redirects.
- Do not force every route to reload.
- Do not introduce a blocking animation between every navigation action.
- Avoid an unstyled flash or visible blank page during route changes.
- Keep route transitions graceful even when the destination loads quickly.

If the existing routing system supports safe client-side page transitions, implement them using its conventions.

If some routes or framework limitations do not support a particular transition, use the best compatible frontend treatment rather than introducing a fragile workaround.

## 3. Animate content sections appropriately

Review the sections on every existing page.

Use consistent section entrance animations where they improve readability.

Suitable patterns may include:

- Fade-in with slight upward movement.
- Subtle staged reveal of related content.
- Small staggered transitions between a section heading and its content.
- Gentle reveals as content enters the viewport.

Do not animate every text line, card, icon, and decorative detail independently.

Group related elements logically.

Use a restrained stagger only where it genuinely improves the presentation.

### Avoid repeated scrolling effects

- Do not repeatedly replay an entrance animation every time the user scrolls a few pixels.
- Do not hide essential content until an animation finishes.
- Do not cause sections to jump in height.
- Do not disrupt scrolling.
- Do not create sticky or parallax effects without a clear purpose.

Prefer a simple and predictable reveal pattern.

## 4. Animate interactive components

Audit component-level interactions across the site.

Where applicable, add or refine animations for:

- Accordions opening and closing.
- Dropdowns appearing and disappearing.
- Tabs switching content.
- Dialogs and modals opening and closing.
- Popovers appearing and dismissing.
- Tooltips appearing.
- Sidebar or navigation expansion.
- Form submission feedback.
- Loading and empty-state transitions.
- Toast notifications appearing and disappearing.
- Cards changing selected state.
- Switches and checkboxes toggling.
- Existing builder controls changing state.

These animations must correspond to genuine component state.

Do not create fake interactions for components that currently serve a different purpose.

Preserve their original functionality and state-management behaviour.

## 5. Preserve animation responsiveness

All transitions must be compatible with rapid user interaction.

For example, if a user quickly opens and closes an accordion, the component should still settle into the correct state.

If navigation changes while an entrance animation is running, the application must remain functional.

If an animation is interrupted, the control must not become stuck in a visually incorrect state.

Use the component's existing state as the source of truth.

