# PHASE 10 — ACCESSIBILITY AND REDUCED MOTION

Premium animation must not reduce accessibility.

## Accessibility requirements

- Use semantic HTML and existing accessible components.
- Preserve keyboard navigation.
- Keep focus indicators visible.
- Maintain appropriate text and control contrast.
- Provide accessible labels for icon-only buttons.
- Preserve form labels and validation announcements.
- Do not communicate important states through colour alone.
- Keep controls understandable to assistive technologies.
- Avoid interactions that require a pointer when a keyboard equivalent should exist.

## Reduced-motion support

Respect the user's `prefers-reduced-motion` preference throughout the application.

When reduced motion is enabled:

- Disable or reduce large page and section movements.
- Avoid continuous decorative animation.
- Reduce or remove staggered entrance effects.
- Preserve meaningful state feedback.
- Keep essential content visible.
- Ensure every control remains fully functional.

Do not disable accessibility preferences just to preserve the visual design.

